65: The graph maps descend in both directions, giving mutually inverse
66: holomorphic automorphisms of \(S\).  This proves the required
67: extension, including II, III and IV fibres.  On an \(I_N\) chain
68: the actual Tate map is multiplication by the section unit and a
69: shift by its component label.  On III and IV, translation acts on
70: the dense smooth parts by addition in the component groups of
71: orders two and three; it therefore permutes the resolved components
72: by those same labels.  Inversion conjugates translation by \(G\)
73: to translation by \(-G\) on the whole surface.
74: 
75: The point \(dG\) is in the identity component at every reducible
76: fibre in the table.  Thus \(M\) has multidegree zero there.
77: The height identity gives
78: \((dG)\cdot O=(d-2)/2\), and its actual divisor class satisfies
79: \[
80:  (t_G)_*[M]-[M]=[F],\qquad t_G^*M\cong M(-F).           \tag{P3K.22.2}
81: \]
82: These equalities are also checked directly in the integer matrices
83: below.  The numerical equality is an equality of line bundles
84: because the rational surface has \(\operatorname{Pic}^0(S)=0\).
85: The resulting Hom line is \(\pi^*\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^1}(1)\).
86: Choose its section with its unique zero at the selected nodal fibre.
87: This gives the actual holomorphic lift with a simple whole-fibre
88: zero.
89: 
90: For an explicit meromorphic frame of that lift, put \(P_j=jG\).
91: On the generic cubic the quotient of the chord through
92: \(P_d,-G\) by the vertical line through \(P_{d-1}\) has divisor
93: \(P_d+(-G)-P_{d-1}-O\).  It is exactly the fibre divisor of
94: \(M\otimes(t_G^*M)^{-1}\).  Its remaining vertical orders form
95: whole fibres, since the component degrees vanish.  Multiplying by
96: the rational base function that moves this degree-one divisor to the
97: chosen \(I_1\) supplies the preceding section.  Thus its frame and
98: all local multipliers are obtained from the stated rational addition
99: map, rather than from an unspecified linearization.
100: 
101: For sufficiently small \(|\lambda|\), multiply this lift by
102: \(\lambda\).  In a fixed Hermitian metric it is uniformly strictly
103: contracting, away from its prescribed zero where the Mumford charts
104: are used.  Quotienting the nonzero line by its iterates is therefore
105: free and proper.  At the zero the actual valuation periods are
106: \((1,0),(0,1)\); the standard vertical-edge fan gives the smooth
107: index-one filling with its one fixed \(\mathbb P^1\), of normal
108: weights \((-1,+1)\).  The chart and line-gluing formulas are the
109: same ones used in P3K.10, with the present holomorphic unit logarithms
110: \(\Gamma,C\).  In particular, on a universal scalar-parameter
111: chart its cusp period and two origins are
112: \[
113:  \begin{aligned}
114:  \gamma&=(\Gamma,C+s+v),\\
115:  \sigma&=(\Gamma v,Cv+(v^2-v)/2),\\
116:  o&=(-\Gamma s,-Cs-s^2/2-sv),
117:  \end{aligned}                                                     \tag{P3K.22.3}
118: \]
119: and the closing ordinary line gauge is \(-s\delta\).  The exact
120: identities
121: \[
122:  \sigma(v+1)-\sigma(v)=\gamma-s\delta,
123:  \quad (\sigma-o)(v+\theta)-(\sigma-o)(v)
124:       =\theta\gamma+\tfrac12\theta(\theta-1)\delta
125: \]
126: hold for these actual units.  The degree-two cusp root used in
127: P3K.10 retains the half-integral ordinary-origin term and its integral
128: deck.  The smooth order-one cap is glued by
129: \((\log q)/(2\pi i)\,\gamma\); its scalar closing gauge is the
130: same actual base function used above.  Thus the gluing is holomorphic
131: in the scalar parameter and closes on its positive loop.  All filled
132: pieces are proper over their base discs; the contraction shell gives
133: properness on their common complement.  The compact base gives
134: compactness.  This establishes the analytic construction and the
135: positive-gamma parameter direction needed for the index calculation.
136: 
137: **Proof: every integer matrix of the source mapping torus.**
138: Use the divisor basis
139: \[
140:  O,F,\{C_{i,j}:1\le j<N_i\},G,
